  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does the ball in a ballpoint do?",
      "a": "The ball rolls as you write. It picks up ink from inside the pen and rolls it onto the paper, and it seals the tip when you stop.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a ballpoint pen get ink onto paper?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the socket do?",
      "a": "The socket is the metal cup at the tip that holds the ball. It lets the ball spin and lets a thin film of ink pass, but keeps the ball from falling out.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does the ball not fall out?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is ballpoint ink so thick?",
      "a": "Thick, oily ink does not leak or blot, and it dries almost at once on paper. Runny ink would pour out past the ball.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does a ballpoint not smudge?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a ballpoint not work upside down?",
      "a": "Its ink needs gravity to flow down to the ball. Upside down, the ink moves away from the ball and air gets in, so it stops writing. Space pens use pressurised ink instead.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is there a hole in a pen?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Who invented the ballpoint pen?",
      "a": "The Hungarian journalist László Bíró and his brother György patented the modern ballpoint in 1938. In many countries a ballpoint is still called a biro.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is a pen called a biro?"
      ]
    }
  ],
